+## Command guard and the careful-full-access mode
+
+`dsh` ships a deletion guard so misparsed destructive commands cannot take your files with them. The host-plane `command-guard` plugin (`packages/guard/careful-full-access`) is active only in the `careful-full-access` sandbox mode — `workspace-write` stays confined by the sandbox itself and `danger-full-access` remains the explicit opt-out. Inside careful mode every `pwsh`/`bash` call passes a zero-cost in-process lexical pre-scan (destructive verb/alias table, cmd-style switches, `.NET` deletion calls, dynamic markers, top-level `git` subcommand dispatch — `git rm --cached` is index-only while `git clean` and `git reset --hard` are flagged) that gates a PowerShell AST pass, and classifies the call into four tiers — **disaster** (drive roots, root wildcards, UNC/extended roots, the user profile, system directories, the workspace root, `Format-*`/`Clear-Disk`/`diskpart clean`, `robocopy /MIR` into a protected root, recursive `.NET` deletion), **elevated** (every delete/format/mirror verb), **normal** (allowed), and **unparseable** (treated as disaster; `iex` never slips through).
+
+Every flagged command runs the review route: deletions first resolve their real scope through a WhatIf dry run (`$WhatIfPreference = $true`, so PowerShell itself expands wildcards/variables/`$env:`) plus a subtree enumeration for recursive directory targets, then a model-check — one bounded call to the session's model that shows the command, its tier, and the reason, and answers three questions (intent, safety, scope). A model that disowns the command denies it outright; an intended-and-safe elevated command runs; disaster-tier commands (and any command the model itself declares dangerous) require human confirmation, red-marked in the approval panel and auto-rejected under approval `never`; an unavailable model-check fails closed as disaster.
+
+Every decision — allow, deny, human-confirm — is audited twice: the complete trail goes to the rotated file log `$DSH_HOME/logs/command-guard.log` (5 MB × 3 copies by default), while the session log keeps a bounded, deduplicated window of `command-guard/decision` events (20 per session, identical commands merged inside a 10-minute TTL).
+
+Under the hood, the `sandbox-windows-acl` backend additionally splits each workspace grant into two ACEs — an inherit-only full-Modify ACE for descendants and a root-only ACE without DELETE/FILE_DELETE_CHILD — so the confined child can delete/rename/git inside the tree but can never delete or rename the workspace root object itself.
+
+## Custom plugins
+
+The two capabilities below are maintained as portable plugin repositories on top of upstream:
+
+- **`dsh-careful-full-access` (command guard + `careful-full-access` sandbox mode)** — described above. Its complete functionality spans core sandbox / filesystem / shell / terminal changes, so the repository ships a companion `patches/careful-full-access.patch` for the harness tree: [dsh-careful-full-access](https://github.com/zdjmrq/dsh-careful-full-access) (tagged `dsh-plugin`).
+- **One-click backend shutdown + frontend refresh that keeps hot plugins** — Settings → General gains "Shut down backend service" and "Refresh frontend" rows (topmost confirm/cancel dialogs), plus F5/Ctrl+R refresh that preserves creation-mode hot plugins: [dsh-restart-plugin](https://github.com/zdjmrq/dsh-restart-plugin) (tagged `dsh-plugin`).
+
+The [dsh-plugin-suite](https://github.com/zdjmrq/dsh-plugin-suite) repository collects both plugins into one cumulative patch against upstream commit `47f943859b` — apply it once and everything is wired:
+
+```sh
+git clone https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness.git
+cd deepseek-harness
+git checkout 47f943859b
+git apply /path/to/dsh-plugin-suite/install.patch
+pnpm install
+pnpm run build
+pnpm dsh web
+```
+
+If your checkout has moved past `47f943859b`, `install.patch` may not apply cleanly — merge by hand instead (the touched points are composition rows, dependency declarations, and tsconfig references).
+
